  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">2025 Cape Verde Floods Rotterdam municipality</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">40,000 people, prioritizing displaced families, host families, and vulnerable groups such as women, children, elderly, and people with disabilities.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">A humanitarian operation led by the Cape Verde Red Cross (CVCV) with IFRC support to assist 40,000 people affected by the August 2025 floods in S&#xe3;o Vicente, Santo Ant&#xe3;o, and parts of S&#xe3;o Nicolau. It provides life-saving relief (food, water, shelter kits, hygiene kits), cash assistance, health and psychosocial support, WASH rehabilitation, and livelihood recovery, while integrating protection, gender, and inclusion and strengthening CVCV&#x2019;s long-term capacity</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The floods caused 12 deaths, 5 missing, and over 27,500 directly affected people. Infrastructure collapsed, including homes, bridges, roads, health centers, and water systems, leaving communities isolated and vulnerable to disease outbreaks and food insecurity. With no permanent international NGOs present, CVCV and IFRC are the main humanitarian actors. The operation addresses urgent humanitarian needs and builds resilience against future climate-related disasters.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The islands of S&#xe3;o Vicente and Santo Ant&#xe3;o, with partial impact in S&#xe3;o Nicolau, Cape Verde.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The response has three phases:

Emergency relief (0&#x2013;3 months): immediate distributions, clean water supply, shelter kits, and psychological first aid.</narrative>
  </description>
